\cls{} and \traj{} both scale positively from 4B to 8B (+1.27\pp{} and +1.70\pp{} respectively), while \cwm{} remains flat ($-$0.03\pp{}). At 8B, the ranking is \cls{} (89.94) $>$ \traj{} (87.70) $>$ \cwm{} (87.33). The \cls{}--\cwm{} gap widens from 1.32\pp{} at 4B to 2.62\pp{} at 8B (Figure~\ref{fig:scaling_gap}); clustered bootstrap on the 3-seed superset (42, 123, 456 for \cls{}/\cwm{}) yields $p = 0.0066$ (95\% CI $[1.80, 2.71]$), which confirms the widening is not a sampling artifact.

\cwm{} flat scaling ($-$0.03\pp{}) may reflect suboptimal hyperparameter transfer at 8B rather than a fundamental limitation; we do not claim generation \emph{cannot} benefit from scale. The interaction between formulation and scale amplifies at repository-level complexity (\S\ref{sec:repo}), where \traj{} also shows no scaling benefit (72.92\% at both 4B and 8B); only \cls{} scales positively across both complexity levels.


\subsection{Per-Test vs.\ Trajectory-Level Equivalence}
\label{sec:equivalence}

We compare \cls{} (aggregated via all-pass rule) against \traj{} on 523 variant-level instances across five seeds.\footnote{Seeds 789 and 0 use NF4 quantized inference; the remaining three use bfloat16.} The pooled gap is $-$0.45\pp{} (95\% CI [$-$1.71, +0.84]); the TOST equivalence test confirms statistical equivalence at $p = 0.007$ ($\pm$2\pp{} margin). The $\pm$2\pp{} margin reflects practical significance: formulation differences below 2\pp{} are unlikely to affect deployment decisions where absolute accuracy dominates the cost-benefit analysis. Within-problem predictions are correlated (intraclass correlation ICC $= 0.191$, $p < 0.0001$, design effect (DEFF) $= 3.97$); all statistical tests use clustered bootstrap accordingly. On DeepSeek-Coder, the observed \cls{}--\cwm{} gap of 0.02\pp{} is consistent with equivalence, but with only 2 seeds the comparison is underpowered. Per-test classification provides diagnostic information (which specific tests fail) at no cost to variant-level accuracy, a result that parallels process supervision~\cite{lightman2023letsverify}, where step-level verification enables error localization without accuracy cost.

Oracle analysis on SWE-bench Verified (500 instances) shows that per-test advantage concentrates at $T \geq 2$ fail-to-pass tests (31.3\% of instances, contributing 55.3\% of the total oracle advantage; Appendix~\ref{app:oracle}).


\subsection{Repo-Level: Complexity-Dependent Divergence}
\label{sec:repo}

Table~\ref{tab:repo_level} compares \cls{} and \cwm{} on SWE-bench Verified per-test classification at repository-level complexity. Checkpoints are val-selected where validation loss is computable (see \S\ref{sec:setup}); the test set ($n{=}9{,}784$) has zero problem overlap with validation. We report both overall accuracy (where unparseable outputs default to the majority class, pass) and parseable-only accuracy to disentangle generation quality from default assignment effects.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Repo-level cross-formulation comparison on SWE-bench Verified test set ($n{=}9{,}784$). \cwm{} unparseable outputs default to pass (majority class). Seeds: Qwen3 primary seed 42; DeepSeek \traj{} 6 seeds.}
\label{tab:repo_level}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{\cls{} Acc (\%)} & \textbf{\cwm{} Acc (\%)} & \textbf{\cwm{} Unparse (\%)} & \textbf{\traj{} Acc (\%)\textsuperscript{$\ddagger$}} \\
\midrule
Qwen3-4B & \textbf{83.63} & 58.04 & 53.70 & 72.92 \\
Qwen3-8B & 84.48 / 85.60\textsuperscript{$\dagger$} & 65.17 & 73.80 & 72.92\textsuperscript{$\diamond$} \\
DeepSeek-6.7B & 82.61 / 74.93\textsuperscript{$\dagger$} & 56.70 & 51.00 & 75.35 $\pm$ 5.00 \\
\midrule
0-shot (Qwen3-8B) & 49.90 & --- & --- & --- \\
0-shot (DeepSeek-6.7B) & 48.50 & --- & 8.70 & --- \\
Majority baseline & \multicolumn{4}{c}{72.96} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}

\vspace{2pt}
{\footnotesize \textsuperscript{$\dagger$}Two configurations: s42 uses drop preprocessing with val-selected checkpoint; s123 uses truncate preprocessing with final epoch (NaN validation loss). The 7.68\pp{} spread conflates seed, preprocessing, and checkpoint selection differences. \textsuperscript{$\ddagger$}\traj{} reports variant-level accuracy ($n{=}48$); \cls{}/\cwm{} columns report per-test accuracy ($n{=}9{,}784$). \cwm{} parseable-only accuracy: Qwen3 42.88\% (4B), 38.52\% (8B); DeepSeek 38.71\% (s42), 41.19\% (s123). All below majority baseline (72.96\%). \cwm{} eval seq\_length: Qwen3 8192, DeepSeek 4096; cross-model \cwm{} values are not directly comparable (\S\ref{sec:repo}). \textsuperscript{$\diamond$}Qwen3-8B \traj{} uses epoch~2 checkpoint (72.92\%); NaN validation loss caused Trainer default to epoch~3 final (70.83\%).}
\end{table}

Base-model prompting (0-shot and few-shot) converges to identical variant-level behavior regardless of strategy: both models classify all unresolved variants correctly and zero resolved variants, collapsing to an always-predict-unresolved baseline (45.83\%, below majority 54.17\%). Per-test accuracy varies widely (46--72\%) but this variation is superficial; variant-level evaluation exposes complete degradation (Appendix~\ref{app:few_shot}).

\paragraph{Formulation divergence.} At repository-level complexity under our 4--8B LoRA protocol, \cls{} and \cwm{} diverge sharply on Qwen3: the gap is 25.59\pp{} at 4B (\cls{} 83.63\% vs.\ \cwm{} 58.04\%, seed 42; Figure~\ref{fig:scaling_gap}) and 19.31\pp{} at 8B (84.48\% vs.\ 65.17\%). Both gaps are an order of magnitude larger than at function level (1--3\pp{}, Table~\ref{tab:scaling}); formulation effectiveness is complexity-dependent. \cwm{} falls below the majority baseline at both scales ($-$14.92\pp{} at 4B, $-$7.79\pp{} at 8B): negative utility for this model/formulation combination.

\paragraph{Cross-model consistency.} DeepSeek-Coder is consistent with a formulation-driven explanation, though single-seed constraints limit causal attribution: both families show \cls{} $>$ \cwm{}, with function$\to$repo degradation of ${\sim}$5/22\pp{} (\cls{}/\cwm{}, Qwen3-8B) and 4--12/25--30\pp{} (DeepSeek-6.7B, \cls{} seed-dependent). The generation bottleneck is consistent across families. DeepSeek \cls{} degradation ranges from ${\sim}$4\pp{} (s42, drop preprocessing) to ${\sim}$12\pp{} (s123, truncate preprocessing), reflecting both preprocessing and checkpoint selection differences (Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy}).

\paragraph{Repo-level failure mode taxonomy.} Variant-level analysis ($n{=}48$, Appendix Table~\ref{tab:variant_level}) reveals three distinct failure modes (Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy}). DeepSeek exhibits both modes depending on training configuration: decision collapse under truncate preprocessing (s123, fail recall ${\sim}$14\%), aggregation penalty under drop preprocessing (s42, fail recall ${\sim}$51\%, variant-level 81.25\%). Decision collapse is configuration-dependent, not model-inherent. \traj{} avoids aggregation penalty by predicting variant outcomes directly (Qwen3: 72.92\% at both 4B and 8B; DeepSeek: 75.35\% $\pm$ 5.00, 6 seeds); Qwen3 data now permits within-model comparison. \cls{} achieves the highest per-test accuracy across configurations, but variant-level performance is configuration-dependent; failure mode is a function of formulation $\times$ model $\times$ configuration $\times$ complexity. To control for training set size asymmetry, we downsample \cls{} training data to match \traj{} (${\sim}$8K function-level instances); downsampled \cls{} achieves 80.74\%, still 7.82\pp{} above \traj{} (72.92\%), confirming that the \cls{} advantage is formulation-driven rather than data-volume-driven (Appendix~\ref{app:downsample}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Repo-level failure mode taxonomy. Variant-level majority baseline: 54.17\%.}
\label{tab:taxonomy}
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\begin{tabular}{llcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Failure Mode} & \textbf{Representative} & \textbf{Fail Recall} & \textbf{Variant Acc} \\
\midrule
Format collapse & \cwm{} (all) & N/A & 48--52\% \\
Decision collapse & \cls{} (DeepSeek, s123) & ${\sim}$14\% & 56\% \\
Aggr.\ penalty & \cls{} (Qwen3-8B) & 51--57\% & 73--81\% \\
Aggr.\ penalty & \cls{} (DeepSeek, s42) & 38--51\% & 79--81\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Default assignment artifact.} The apparent \cwm{} ``improvement'' from 4B (58.04\%) to 8B (65.17\%) is an artifact: as unparseable rate increases from 53.7\% to 73.8\%, more predictions default to the majority class (pass, 72.96\%), inflating accuracy. Parseable-only accuracy \emph{decreases} from 42.88\% (4B) to 38.52\% (8B). Gold mutation (unmodified code) parse rate collapses from 43.5\% to 23.9\%; even the simplest generation target exhibits a fundamental bottleneck (Appendix~\ref{app:repo_mutation}).

